cff-version: 1.2.0
message: >-
  If you use this software, please cite it using the metadata in this file. This is an unofficial Rust port of the
  OHDSI Data Quality Dashboard: please also cite the upstream paper listed under references.
title: "dqd: a Rust port of the OHDSI Data Quality Dashboard"
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Borakati
    given-names: Aditya
    # orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0000-0000-0000"   # add your ORCID before the first release
repository-code: "https://github.com/ABorakati/data-quality-dashboard-rs"
license: Apache-2.0
# Keep in step with `version` in Cargo.toml and the release tag (v<version>) when you cut a release.
version: 2.9.0
# date-released: "YYYY-MM-DD"                          # set at release time
# doi: "10.5281/zenodo.NNNNNNN"                        # the Zenodo concept DOI, once the first release is archived
abstract: >-
  A Rust implementation of the OHDSI Data Quality Dashboard 2.9.0 for OMOP common data model databases. It runs the
  upstream data quality checks natively against SQLite, DuckDB and PostgreSQL, with no R or Java runtime, and writes
  the same results as the pinned upstream R package. The port is checked against that package on the Eunomia sample
  datasets. It is not affiliated with or endorsed by OHDSI.
keywords:
  - OMOP
  - OHDSI
  - data quality
  - common data model
  - observational health data
  - Rust
references:
  - type: article
    title: "Increasing trust in real-world evidence through evaluation of observational data quality"
    authors:
      - family-names: Blacketer
        given-names: Clair
      - family-names: DeFalco
        given-names: Frank J.
      - family-names: Ryan
        given-names: Patrick B.
      - family-names: Rijnbeek
        given-names: Peter R.
    journal: "Journal of the American Medical Informatics Association"
    year: 2021
    volume: 28
    issue: 10
    start: 2251
    end: 2257
    doi: "10.1093/jamia/ocab132"
  - type: software
